WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.440
Let's see who else we can talk to.&nbsp;
How about Mark from Framingham?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.520
Mark from Framingham. You're on the air.
Hi, can you hear me? Yes, I can. Mark, hi.&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.160
You recently expressed a wariness about&nbsp;
blanket student debt cancelation, and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.200
just wondering what your reasons for that are.
Yeah. So I've kind of outlined it before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.960
but I'm glad to sort of do it again. I think&nbsp;
that there should be some debt cancelation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.920
I'm sort of open to hearing different ideas about&nbsp;
what level of debt cancelation that should be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.240
I think the problem with just a blanket&nbsp;
debt cancelation for everybody, period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.840
is that it is going to do a couple of different&nbsp;
things that I don't think are particularly good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:49.520
One is there should be some element of it being&nbsp;
need based and need based. I would define it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.680
probably very differently than than some others.&nbsp;
And my threshold for what is need based would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.080
significant. A lot of people would fall under&nbsp;
that. But I don't see a reason to just go in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.960
and cancel all the debt of everybody who went&nbsp;
to really expensive schools because that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.640
what they chose to do. They could have gone to a&nbsp;
cheaper school. They could afford to go to a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.000
expensive school, took on some debt because that's&nbsp;
where they wanted to go. Could have ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.520
without debt at a cheaper school. And we're just&nbsp;
going to say here, just take take all the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.040
and pay it off. I also think that there's people&nbsp;
who have debt forgiveness programs based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.160
fields that they are in, if they work a certain&nbsp;
amount of time in a certain underserved area or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.560
whatever the case may be. So we're sort of saying&nbsp;
like, let's throw even more money at a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:42.320
that already has a path to having some degree or&nbsp;
even full debt forgiveness there. And it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:49.680
seem to me to make sense to say everyone, student&nbsp;
debt period is forgiven. I want it to be slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:55.120
more targeted than that. I want it to be more than&nbsp;
10 grand. But I don't think it's logical to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.680
just pay off everybody's debt, period. I've not&nbsp;
seen a good argument as to why we would do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.040
You are still to there should be some&nbsp;
responsibility. Like, listen, it's beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.600
your control that some schools now cost 70 K.&nbsp;
That's insane. The reason schools cost 70 K&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.880
is because it's so easy to get student loans so&nbsp;
the school knows, hey, we'll go from 65 to 70 K&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.840
because it's easy to get student loans. We'll get&nbsp;
the money. Then it'll be their problem to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.520
out figure out how to pay it off. Like that's&nbsp;
why I want some student loan debt forgiveness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:34.640
But to say full, total and complete just doesn't&nbsp;
really make sense to me economically, culturally.&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.920
I've not seen a good argument for it. OK,&nbsp;
would you mind if I may be shared a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.280
my perspective, I'm sure. No, please, please.
So the way I see it is a lot of people talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.160
about how people who hold a lot of student debt&nbsp;
tend to be, on average, higher income because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:58.160
college graduates on average in the aggregate make&nbsp;
more money. But the way I see it is that higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:04.720
income at some point doesn't necessarily mean that&nbsp;
much. If you also have more debt, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.360
medical school debt on average on top of&nbsp;
undergraduate is two hundred fifty K for law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.760
school, it's one hundred fifty K. And it's kind&nbsp;
of like if I run a business and I want to expand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.440
I may have increased revenues,&nbsp;
but I also have increased costs.&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.720
Yeah. And in fact that this has been studied&nbsp;
and while there is an income premium to college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.880
degrees, the net worth premium is significantly&nbsp;
smaller because you make more money, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.520
have more debt. Debt cancels out some of the more&nbsp;
money you have. So, yes, you're absolutely right.&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.640
The other thing I would point out is a lot of&nbsp;
college graduates, just because they have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.280
go where the jobs are, they live in high cost&nbsp;
of living areas. So, you know, a hundred grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:55.200
in New York City, according to CNN, Money, is&nbsp;
equivalent to 40 K in Houston. So so it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:02.960
sound a lot on paper, but day to day, it's not&nbsp;
really that effect. So and personally, for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:11.280
I tend to lean more towards maybe helping too&nbsp;
many people or otherwise helping people who maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.880
don't need that help rather than risk not helping&nbsp;
people who do need that. I totally agree with you.&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.840
That's my view with all of these&nbsp;
social welfare programs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.680
you will always be able to find someone who got a&nbsp;
benefit that you could argue didn't really deserve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.480
it. And I want the line to be where there's a&nbsp;
few people getting it that don't deserve it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.680
long as everybody that does is getting it. And&nbsp;
so, for example, I'm very open to the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:43.600
there's a set amount of debt cancelation plus&nbsp;
a multiplier based on some combination of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.240
is your income and where do you live in&nbsp;
cost of. Like, I'm totally up for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.240
It doesn't make sense to me to just say we're&nbsp;
canceling everybody's debt regardless of how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.960
it is, regardless of how much you make, regardless&nbsp;
regardless of where you live, how long ago you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.120
obtained the debt, how much you know, all this&nbsp;
that I'm not there, but I'm definitely at let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.760
let's we've got to do more than 10k, OK?
Yeah, I definitely understand where you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.200
coming from. I had one last question, though.&nbsp;
Sure. You've had people such as Steve Keen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.760
and Stephanie Kelton as well as Dean Baker on.&nbsp;
Yes. These are people who are very critical of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.280
economic consensus. And I think you've expressed&nbsp;
why we should be skeptical of a lot of mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:31.440
economics. However, I also think that especially&nbsp;
with covid and climate change, we should listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:38.080
expert consensus on those in those areas.&nbsp;
So is that inconsistent on my part to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.520
skeptical of the economics profession, but also be&nbsp;
trusting of the consensus in the natural science?&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.920
No, I don't think so. I don't think so. And&nbsp;
many check out my interview with Israeli&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:55.440
economist Ariel Rubenstein, who recently explained&nbsp;
economics is a study of what economists believe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:04.880
It's not like, for example, rocket science,&nbsp;
climate science, immunology. So, no, I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.000
perfectly reasonable to look at economic thinking&nbsp;
in a different way than we look at, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.400
vaccine vaccine science. I think you're right&nbsp;
to look at them as two different things.&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.720
OK, yeah, it's also worth noting that, like I was&nbsp;
listening to what you said earlier about cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.040
culture, it's also worth noting because a lot&nbsp;
of conservatives say like, oh, universities are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.800
left leaning, but a lot of billionaires donate a&nbsp;
lot of money to universities with the expectation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:37.440
that they hire intellectuals who will, for lack&nbsp;
of a better term, serve as mouthpieces for them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.000
Like Koch brother, like the Koch brothers,&nbsp;
donates a lot of money to MIT. Yeah. And MIT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.360
then becomes reluctant to hire left leaning&nbsp;
economists. So, yeah, it was great to talk to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.880
you again. I really appreciate you taking my call&nbsp;
because I really appreciate speaking with you.&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.680
All right. Thanks very much. Great to hear from&nbsp;
you. That is going to do it for calls today.&nbsp;

